hrough the wind profile: about 6.2 at 100 m from 32\u00b0, 4.7 at 40 m from 52\u00b0, and 6.3 at 150 m from 24\u00b0. Direction turns counterclockwise with height, roughly -25\u00b0 per 100 m, with the lower segment noisy. So the veer is around -25, and the direction at 100 m sits near 32\u00b0. Next I'm turning to the shear.\n\nNow I'm estimating the shear exponent from the speed ratios, which comes out to roughly 0.2 and looks noisy. Next I want to run a few more tests at 70 and 30 meters, along with some smoke drops, and try a comparison to fit the drag."}, {"before_call": 6, "text": "The predicted fall times for the 100 and 150 cases come out longer, which points to drag being too high. I'll try lowering the drag coefficient to 0.035."}, {"before_call": 7, "text": "I've got the direction backwards: more drag stretches the time, so I need to bring the drag down to 0.026."}, {"before_call": 8, "text": "I'm testing dt at 0.028, since 0.03 gave mixed results and 0.026 gave all negative values."}, {"before_call": 9, "text": "The wind estimate looks about right, though the leftover vertical error hints the wind may run a bit more east than I assumed, and I'm not sure about its direction at higher altitude yet. I'd like more altitude samples, say at 30 and 70. Next I want a low-altitude pass straight through the center of the smoke, heading east from the western starting point to test the updraft, with the fall taking roughly four seconds.\n\nI'm working out the release point for a low-altitude drop at 100 m with speed 50, since the earlier test only drifted about 95 m in x and the wind pushes the load north. I'll set the release at (-95, -20) so it should land near the center, then run a second drop at 40 m from (-75, -9)."}, {"before_call": 11, "text": "I'm checking how the fall time changed: it went up by 1.2 s for one case and 0.4 s for the other, so I'll fit the updraft to those numbers."}, {"before_call": 12, "text": "The updraft looks too weak at 3, with dt at -